residual structure, parameter correlations, chemistry, bounds, and
unresolved peaks.
2.2
Automation of XRD Analysis
Crystallographic engines such as FullProf [28] and GSAS-II [42]
provide mature numerical capabilities. Automation has progressed
from high-throughput wrappers (SrRietveld) [39], to expert systems
(AutoFP) [10], reinforcement learning (PowderBot) [13], black-box
optimization (BBO-Rietveld) [25], derivative-guided recipe selection
[41], and learned parameter prediction [24]. Robotic PXRD systems
have integrated sample preparation, measurement, and automated
analysis [49].
Phase identification is related but distinct. Deep models can
learn multi-phase labels from synthetic patterns [18, 31]; XCA
performs autonomous probabilistic phase identification without
a language agent [23]; CrystalShift uses symmetry-constrained
pseudo-refinement and Bayesian comparison [7]; Dara searches
and refines multiple phase combinations [12]; and RADAR-PD
couples mismatch-tolerant prediction with Rietveld verification
[46]. Recent end-to-end pipelines broaden this scope: AGAPI-XRD
combines database matching, a fine-tuned structure generator, and
GSAS-II/BGMN refinement [4], while MatDiffract couples hierar-
chical retrieval with full-pattern refinement and quantitative phase
fitting [44]. COD [14], opXRD [16], SimXRD-4M [5], and SIMPOD
[27] improve data availability. These advances automate engines,
prediction, or search. Our focus is complementary: constraining
and evaluating the sequence of decisions made by a language agent
operating scientific software.
Table 1 compares five language agents by their supported XRD
modules, callable scientific resources, and evaluation scope. The
multimodal LLM-CSP agent combines pattern predictors, a knowl-
edge graph, and search for structure and space-group classification,
but does not execute iterative refinement [6]. The AI X-ray Scientist
closes the loop over detector observations and motor commands
for single-crystal beamline alignment [9]. Rongzai and AgentBuild-
Rietveld operate GSAS-II for powder refinement and fit diagno-
sis, whereas NeuDiff connects neutron-data reduction, structure
refinement, validation, and provenance [19, 32, 45]. Their evalua-
tions remain specialized: five neutron-powder tasks for Rongzai,
eight X-ray cases for AgentBuild, and one repeated reference work-
flow for NeuDiff. OPENXRD provides a complementary non-agent
benchmark of 217 crystallography questions over 74 language and
multimodal models, but does not test scientific software execution
[43]. XRDBench unifies these two evaluation needs through 100
diagnostic tasks and 34 executable powder-diffraction workflows
across eleven families, evaluated with ten LLM agents under one
protocol.
2.3
Tool-Using and Scientific Agents
ReAct interleaves language reasoning with actions [48], while Tool-
former learns when and how to call APIs [30]. AgentBench [20],
𝜏-bench [47], MLAgentBench [17], and DSAEval [37] show that
endpoint success alone can hide long-horizon failures and incon-
sistency. Scientific-agent work includes chemistry agents [3], au-
tonomous laboratory orchestration [2], and broad research automa-
tion [21, 33–36]. ScienceAgentBench argues for authentic, task-level
assessment before claims of end-to-end discovery [8].
AutoXRD applies this lesson to a narrow scientific domain
with unusually sharp validity constraints. It differs from generic
agents by making action schemas, falsifiable predictions, stage or-
der, coupled-parameter exclusions, mandatory physical checks, and
rejected steps part of the executable protocol.
